> Principles for understanding climate risks and potential for adaptation investment
> Climate risks are multifaceted, emerging from both acute shocks and gradual trends and cascading and simultaneous events. Realised impacts come from the interaction between hazards, population and capital exposed, and multiple forms of socioeconomic vulnerability. While climate change continues to disrupt the world, economies will continue to evolve.
> 
> Adaptation actions encompass both reactive and anticipatory strategies designed to reduce the physical climate risks. Reactive adaptations, such as household use of air conditioning during heatwaves and last-minute irrigation adjustments in response to drought, are typically immediate responses to climate stressors. In contrast, anticipatory adaptations involve proactive and strategic initiatives like investing in coastal defences, implementing comprehensive soil management practices, and committing to managed retreat from vulnerable areas. Such actions require extensive planning and potentially substantial capital investment. Importantly, many of these adaptation choices have consequences that extend far beyond the specific locales where they are enacted, influencing broader ecological, economic, and social systems.
> 
> We are mainly concerned with policy-driven adaptation, but this is only part of the necessary action. Along with any public action, individuals and firms will make adaptation investments for their private benefit. Rising incomes in many regions will also confer significant benefits, in the form of reduced vulnerability and greater adaptive capacity. To date, most documented adaptation strategies and their benefits have been fragmented and localized, with limited assessment of risk reduction (Berrang-Ford et al., 2021). However, even when private action is possible, it may be overpriced and under-invested. In fact, adaptation is more complex than mitigation finance because of the challenges to define, evaluate and scale investments. Policy-driven adaptation investment is crucial both for achieving economic goals and for creating financial incentives that encourage further adaptation investment (Gautman et al., 2024). 
> 
> One of those economic goals is to take advantage of all of the actions that will provide more benefit than they will cost to implement. This is a basic economic tenet: both economic growth and the greatest good can often be pursued just by identifying actions where benefits exceed costs. 
> 
> The benefits of adaptation are generally not restricted to financial returns. Many climate impacts reduce welfare, a composite used by economists to reflect impacts on anything people value, more than they reduce economic production. For example, the heightened risk of health problems from heat waves represents a significant loss of welfare, even if the elderly who are most at risk do not directly contribute to the economy. Because we include these “non-market” risks and the non-market benefits of adaptation, governments can only expect a portion of the benefits to accrue to their balance sheets. The value added to people’s lives is real, nonetheless, and creative policies can capture part of this value in monetary terms and still leave people better off  (e.g., health benefits translating to increased productivity and tax receipts).
> 
> To make welfare damages more comprehensible, they are often translated into a percent of GDP loss, which indicates the extent of welfare loss in a comparable unit. For example, a welfare damage of 14% of GDP-equivalent suggests that the loss in welfare corresponds to what would occur if 14% of consumption were removed from individuals’ lives. It is important to note that this method of normalization does not imply that these welfare losses directly translate into a reduction in GDP; rather, it provides a framework for understanding the significant impacts of non-market factors on human well-being, emphasising that the non-economic effects can be just as damaging as measurable declines in economic output.
> 
> The understanding of the relationship between welfare damages, adaptation, and mitigation has a long history. Watkiss et al. (2015) show that welfare damages as a percentage of global GDP are lowest when adaptation and mitigation strategies are combined and implemented simultaneously.
> 
> The final chapter of this report develops a case for action, building on basic principles that rationale are well-known:
> 
> First, national-level adaptation should be driven by robust policy frameworks, and clear governance structures establish risk ownership and set out objectives and goals, which help prioritise actions that combine high potential impact with the greatest urgency. It involves developing strategic pathways that integrate adaptation into broader development and economic plans.
> 
> Second, adaptation needs to be seen as a process. The inherent uncertainties in climate projections and risks necessitate decision-making approaches that are flexible, adaptive, and precautionary. Policies should incorporate adaptive management techniques to adjust actions as new information becomes available.
> 
> Third, adaptation strategies must be tailored to specific local contexts, accounting for unique geographic, cultural, and economic characteristics. Engaging local communities and stakeholders in the planning and implementation processes helps to ensure that solutions are relevant, effective, and supported by those directly affected.
> 
> This report does not intend to provide analysis on how to prioritize specific adaptation strategies to maximize welfare; rather, it offers a useful review of the benefits of adaptation at a macroscale. Moreover, it does not focus on within-country heterogeneity in climate change scenarios, which could guide sector-specific adaptation investment.
